# Defect-Tracking Store [SPEC:CXC-DEFECT-STORE]

> The persistence layer for defect investigations. Born 2026-07-23 from the subagent-chip bug that was
> "fixed" twice ([DEC-SUBAGENT-CHIP-FORK-DIVERGENCE]) and the agent built to stop that
> ([DEC-DEFECT-INVESTIGATOR-AGENT]). The lesson: a defect that recurs is a PROCESS signal, and a process
> signal needs a durable, QUERYABLE home — one that answers "how often is the root cause under-spec vs a
> fork?" (an aggregate) AND "has THIS defect happened before, and what does it exemplify?" (a graph). Those
> are two different data shapes, so the store is deliberately two-part: SQLite-primary + LTS-complement.

- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-RETRO]
- pinned-to: [DEC-DEFECT-TRACKING-STORE]
- status: draft — David 2026-07-23 (live), spec-first per [DEC-SUBAGENT-CHIP-FORK-DIVERGENCE]'s lesson
- premise: David — SQLite-primary for the aggregate signal, LTS-complement for the recurrence graph. The
  histogram of root causes is the loudest process lever (it says which discipline to tighten); the recurrence
  relationships are what a table cannot hold (a defect SUPERSEDES a prior fix, EXEMPLIFIES a decision/wart).

### [CXC-DS-SPLIT] Why two stores — the shapes are different
- The AGGREGATE question ("what fraction of defects are under-spec? is fork-divergence trending down after we
  started de-forking?") is a `GROUP BY` over uniform rows — SQLite's native shape, cheap to roll up, cheap to
  chart. Every investigation writes exactly one such row.
- The RECURRENCE question ("has this exact defect been fixed before; what earlier fix did it invalidate; what
  decision/wart does it exemplify") is a GRAPH — typed edges between named nodes. A flat table cannot express
  "[DEFECT-A] SUPERSEDES the fix asserted in [DEFECT-B]" without inventing join tables; the decision-LTS
  already does typed edges natively. So significant/recurring defects ALSO get a [DEFECT-*] node.
- Division of labour: EVERY investigation -> one SQLite row (even trivial ones — the histogram wants them all).
  SIGNIFICANT or RECURRING ones ALSO get a [DEFECT-*] node (the graph stays curated, not noisy). The SQLite
  row's `prior_fix_ref` / `spec_added` are pointers; the [DEFECT-*] node is where the reasoning lives.

### [CXC-DS-SQLITE] The SQLite-primary table — `defect_investigations` (in the retrospective metrics.db)
- Lives in the SAME `intelligence/retrospective/data/metrics.db` as the other capture-types (one store, one
  connect()), added ADDITIVELY alongside `away_watch` etc. Columns mirror the defect-investigator agent's
  final-text deliverable, one row per run:
  - `id` — PK autoincrement. `ts` — epoch seconds of the investigation.
  - `symptom` — the SHORT expected-vs-observed (step 1 CHARACTERIZE). `area` — the area/file the defect lives in.
  - `root_cause_class` — the taxonomy (step 5), normalized to exactly one of
    `under-spec | oversight | fork-divergence | spec-drift | other` (the agent's `implementation-oversight`
    collapses to `oversight`; unknown -> `other`, conservative, never dropped).
  - `recurrence` (0/1) + `prior_fix_ref` — the loudest signal (step 2): was this "fixed" before, and where.
  - `under_spec` (0/1), `fork` (0/1) — the two structural traps (steps 3/4), kept as flags so the rollup can
    slice on them independently of the single root_cause_class.
  - `spec_added` — a reference to the spec node/file the fix added (the harvest-signal spec-defect route).
  - `test_added` (0/1) — did the fix carry the enforcing test that closes the CLASS, not just the instance.
  - `resolution` — a short human summary. `branch` — the `defect/<slug>` branch the proposal lives on.
- API (in `intelligence/retrospective/db.py`): `record_defect_investigation(...)` (APPEND — each investigation
  is a distinct event like `token_spikes`, returns the new row id); `list_defect_investigations(since=None)`;
  and `defects_by_root_cause(since=None)`.
- `defects_by_root_cause(since)` is THE process-improvement signal: a `{root_cause_class: count}` histogram
  ordered count-desc (tallest bar first). A tall `under-spec` bar says "spec-first harder"; a tall
  `fork-divergence` bar says "de-fork by reference". `since` windows it to a recent arc so improvement is
  visible over time. This is a RAW TALLY, no verdict — consistent with the metrics store's hard boundary.

### [CXC-DS-LTS] The LTS-complement — the `[DEFECT-*]` node shape + its typed edges
- A `[DEFECT-*]` node lives in the decision corpus (`decisions/decision-graph-composeux-architect.lts`, or a
  dedicated `decisions/defects.lts` as the corpus grows) and captures ONE significant/recurring defect the way
  [DEC-SUBAGENT-CHIP-FORK-DIVERGENCE] captured the chip bug — the reasoning, not just the fact.
- PREFIX: `DEFECT-` — a new registered prefix in this domain (ComposeUX Composer, CXC- corpus). A `[DEFECT-*]`
  node is a defect INSTANCE record (distinct from a `[DEC-*]` decision node and a `wart` note). Slug convention:
  `[DEFECT-<AREA>-<SHORT>]`, e.g. `[DEFECT-SUBAGENT-CHIP-PRUNE]`.
- Node properties (mirroring the decision-node shape): `lifecycle-position` (investigated | fixed | verified),
  `investigated-in-session`, `importance`, `symptom`, `root-cause-class` (same taxonomy as the column),
  `recurrence` (bool), `resolution`, `branch`, `enforcing-test` (the test that closes the class).
- THE THREE TYPED EDGES (this is what the table cannot hold):
  - `relates-to` -> **RELATES_TO**: the neighbouring decisions/specs/other defects in the same area (the
    same edge the decision corpus already uses; RELATED_TO in the FactLattice model).
  - `supersedes` -> **SUPERSEDES**: the PRIOR fix this defect's investigation invalidated. A recurrence
    ALWAYS carries a `supersedes` edge to the earlier [DEFECT-*] (or the commit's decision node) whose fix
    didn't hold — that edge IS the recurrence chain, walkable to see how many times an area has re-broken.
  - `exemplifies` -> **EXEMPLIFIES**: the general decision / pattern / wart this defect is a concrete instance
    of (e.g. a fork-divergence defect EXEMPLIFIES [DX-NARROW-WAIST]'s "reuse by reference, never fork", and
    EXEMPLIFIES the wart in [DEC-SUBAGENT-CHIP-FORK-DIVERGENCE]). This is the edge that turns a pile of bugs
    into evidence FOR a principle — the histogram says "how much", the EXEMPLIFIES edges say "of what".

### [CXC-DS-EXAMPLE] Canonical shape — the chip bug as a [DEFECT-*] node (illustrative)
- The 2026-07-22 chip bug, already captured as the DECISION [DEC-SUBAGENT-CHIP-FORK-DIVERGENCE], is the
  reference for the INSTANCE shape. A [DEFECT-*] node for it would read:
```
### [DEFECT-SUBAGENT-CHIP-PRUNE] Done-chips didn't disappear — recurred (2nd fix) via un-spec'd + forked prune
- lifecycle-position: fixed
- investigated-in-session: 2026-07-22
- importance: high
- symptom: completed subagent chips remained on the console instead of pruning after done.
- root-cause-class: fork-divergence
- recurrence: true
- resolution: de-fork the prune to one shared subagent-chip model both widgets consume by reference.
- branch: defect/subagent-chip-prune
- enforcing-test: cross-widget acceptance parametrized over whatever renders self/subagents.
- supersedes: [COMMIT-20856df]        # the morning fix on subagent_ledger that didn't hold (fork A)
- exemplifies: [DX-NARROW-WAIST], [DEC-SUBAGENT-CHIP-FORK-DIVERGENCE]
- relates-to: [DEC-INTELLIGENCE-WIDGET-CONSOLIDATION], [CXC-ICV-SUBAGENTS]
```
- Recording that SAME defect in SQLite is one `record_defect_investigation(symptom="done-chips don't
  disappear", area="intelligence/console/intelligence_card.py", root_cause_class="fork-divergence",
  recurrence=True, prior_fix_ref="20856df", under_spec=True, fork=True, spec_added="[CXC-ICV-SUBAGENTS]",
  test_added=True, branch="defect/subagent-chip-prune")` — the row feeds the histogram; the node holds the why.

### [CXC-DS-DECISION] Why this shape — the reasoning to preserve
- change: Adopt a two-part defect store (SQLite-primary aggregate + LTS-complement [DEFECT-*] graph) rather
  than a single store of either kind.
- context_for_change:
    prior_state: "Defects were captured ad hoc — a code comment, at best a one-off [DEC-*] node like the chip
      bug. No queryable aggregate; no consistent recurrence edge. The chip bug recurred partly because its
      first fix left no durable, findable record that it had been a spec/fork problem."
    reasoning: "The two questions defects must answer have different shapes. 'What class of defect dominates?'
      is an aggregate GROUP BY — SQLite. 'Has this recurred, and what does it exemplify?' is a typed graph —
      the decision-LTS. Forcing both into one store cripples one of them: a table can't walk a recurrence
      chain; a hand-authored graph can't be charted or windowed cheaply and would drown in trivial defects."
    rejected_alternative: "LTS-only (a [DEFECT-*] node for EVERY defect) — drowns the curated graph in noise
      and makes the histogram a manual count. SQLite-only — loses the SUPERSEDES/EXEMPLIFIES recurrence graph,
      re-creating the exact blindness that let the chip bug recur."
    counter_argument: "Two stores risk drift — a defect in SQLite with no node, or a node with no row."
    counter_response: "Accepted and bounded: EVERY investigation writes the SQLite row (cheap, mandatory);
      only significant/recurring ones ALSO get a node (curated). The row's prior_fix_ref/spec_added are the
      pointers back; the node is authored during the same architect review that records the row."
    invalidating_assumption: "That defect volume stays low enough for the [DEFECT-*] graph to be hand-curated.
      If defects become high-volume, revisit: auto-generate skeleton nodes from the SQLite rows, or promote
      to the full DELTA machinery (SPEC §8.3)."
    implication: "db.py gains defect_investigations + record/list/rollup APIs (additive); the DEFECT- prefix
      is registered for the CXC- corpus; the defect-investigator agent's write-back targets both halves."
- relates-to: [DEC-DEFECT-TRACKING-STORE], [DEC-DEFECT-INVESTIGATOR-AGENT], [DEC-SUBAGENT-CHIP-FORK-DIVERGENCE]

### [CXC-DS-OPEN] Open / glass
- The [DEFECT-*] write-back (who authors the node, and whether the defect-investigator drafts a skeleton the
  architect ratifies) is wired by David — this spec defines the SHAPE, not the automation.
- A surfaced process-improvement view (the histogram over time, per-area recurrence chains) is a later
  ComposeUX surface, deferred; the rollup API is the seam it will read.
